## How to Choose the Right Therapist for You

Finding an available therapist is only the first step.

You also want to consider whether the therapist's experience and approach fit what you're looking for.

### Think about your reason for seeking therapy

Are you dealing with anxiety? Relationship concerns? Grief? Trauma? Depression? A major life transition?

You don't need to diagnose yourself, but having a general idea of what prompted you to seek support can help you communicate your needs.

### Ask about their approach

Different therapists use different therapeutic approaches.

You can ask:

**"What approach do you typically use with someone experiencing concerns like mine?"**

A therapist should be able to explain their approach in language you can understand.

### Consider the relationship

You don't have to know immediately whether a therapist is the perfect fit.

The first few sessions can help you determine whether you feel comfortable communicating with the therapist and whether you feel that your goals are being understood.

### Think practically

A therapist may be an excellent clinical fit, but if the location makes it extremely difficult to attend appointments consistently, that matters too.

Sustainable therapy needs to fit into your real life.

## Common Misconceptions About In-Person Therapy

### "In-person therapy is always more effective."

There is not enough evidence to support that blanket statement.

Research comparing video-based and face-to-face psychotherapy has found similar outcomes across a range of studies, although the evidence varies by condition and treatment.

### "I need to know exactly what to talk about before my first appointment."

You don't.

Part of the therapist's role is helping you identify what matters and organize the concerns you want to address.

### "Going to therapy means something is seriously wrong with me."

Seeking counseling does not mean you are weak or that something is fundamentally wrong with you.

People seek therapy for many reasons, including personal growth, relationship challenges, stress, grief, life changes, and mental health concerns.

### "I have to stay with the first therapist I meet."

You can ask questions and consider whether the therapist is a good fit.

If something does not feel right, you can discuss your concerns with the therapist or explore other professional options.

## When Professional Support May Be Useful

You may want to consider talking with a mental health professional if emotional or psychological concerns are interfering with your daily life, relationships, work, school, sleep, or ability to manage everyday responsibilities.

You do not have to wait until you reach a breaking point.

A therapist can help you explore what you are experiencing and determine whether counseling may be appropriate.

If you are experiencing an immediate mental health emergency or believe you may hurt yourself or someone else, seek urgent help through local emergency services or call/text **988** in the United States.

This article is educational and does not diagnose or treat any mental health condition. A qualified healthcare or mental health professional can provide guidance based on your individual circumstances.

